= i
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Let $c:\mathbb N\to\{1,\ldots,r\}$ be the given <finite colouring>, with $\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$. Give each unordered pair $\{a,b\}$ the colour $c(a+b)$. The infinite <Ramsey theorem for r-sets>, in its two-element case, gives an infinite <homogeneous set for a colouring> $X$. Enumerate it as $x_1<x_2<\cdots$. Every distinct-index sum $x_i+x_j$ has the common pair colour, so
$$
\boxed{\{x_i+x_j:i\ne j\}\text{ is monochromatic}.}
$$
This application of the infinite <Ramsey theorem for r-sets> uses only unordered pairs.

= ii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For the <finite colouring> $c$, colour each unordered pair $\{a,b\}$, written with $a<b$, by $c(a+2b)$. The infinite <Ramsey theorem for r-sets> again gives an infinite <homogeneous set for a colouring>. If its increasing enumeration is $x_1<x_2<\cdots$, then
$$
\boxed{\{x_i+2x_j:i<j\}\text{ is monochromatic}.}
$$
The ordering in this pair colouring is essential: it assigns the coefficient $2$ to the larger member.

= iii
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the <alternating dyadic colouring excludes symmetric weighted pair sums> construction:
$$
\boxed{c(n)=\lfloor\log_2 n\rfloor\pmod2.}
$$
Thus consecutive <dyadic intervals> $[2^k,2^{k+1})$ have opposite colours. Suppose an increasing infinite <sequence> $X=(x_j)$ had all its distinct-index weighted sums in one <colour class>. <Set> $\delta(y)=2^{\lfloor\log_2 y\rfloor+1}-y$, the distance to the next strictly larger power of $2$.

If $\delta$ is unbounded on $X$, fix a term $x$ and choose a later term $y>2x$ with $\delta(y)>2x$. Put $L=2^{\lfloor\log_2 y\rfloor}$, so $y=2L-\delta(y)$. Then
$$
L\le y+2x<2L,\qquad 2L\le2y+x=4L-2\delta(y)+x<4L.
$$
These two weighted sums lie in adjacent <dyadic intervals> and therefore have opposite colours.

If $\delta$ is bounded by $D$ on $X$, choose a term $x>2D$ and a later term $y>2x$. With the same $L$, we have
$$
2L<y+2x=2L-\delta(y)+2x<4L,
\qquad
4L<2y+x=4L-2\delta(y)+x<8L.
$$
For the upper bounds, $y+2x<2y<4L$ and $2y+x<\tfrac52y<5L<8L$. These weighted sums also lie in adjacent <dyadic intervals>. In both cases they are $x_i+2x_j$ and $x_j+2x_i$ for two distinct indices, contradicting the common <colour class>. Hence **the symmetric distinct-index conclusion is false**, even for this two-colour <finite colouring>.

= iv
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Every <ultrafilter> contains exactly one cell of any finite <set partition>. Indeed, two disjoint cells cannot both belong to a proper <filter>, while if none belonged to the <ultrafilter>, all their complements would belong to it and their finite <intersection> would be empty.

Apply this property to the two <colour classes> in (iii). One belongs to any proposed <ultrafilter>, but neither contains a symmetric weighted-pair configuration from an increasing infinite <sequence>. This contradicts the proposed property of every member. Therefore **no such <ultrafilter> exists**.

= 2
{parent=Paper 14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

<Van der Waerden theorem> says that for every pair of positive <integers> $r,k$ there is $W(r,k)$ such that every <finite colouring> of $[W(r,k)]=\{1,\ldots,W(r,k)\}$ using at most $r$ colours contains a <monochromatic> <arithmetic progression> of length $k$ and positive <common difference>. Consequently every <finite colouring> of $\mathbb N$ contains arbitrarily long <monochromatic> <arithmetic progressions>.

Here is a <colour-focusing proof of Van der Waerden theorem>. Induct on $k$, simultaneously for all $r$. For $k=1$, take $W(r,1)=1$. Suppose $W(R,k)$ exists for every number of colours $R$, and fix $r$. We will construct finite bounds $F_t$, $1\le t\le r$, with the following property: every <finite colouring> of $[F_t]$ using at most $r$ colours contains either a <monochromatic> length-$(k+1)$ <arithmetic progression>, or $t$ length-$k$ <arithmetic progressions> of pairwise distinct colours, all focused at the same point $f\in[F_t]$. Specifically, their terms are
$$
f-d_i,f-2d_i,\ldots,f-kd_i,\qquad d_i>0,\quad 1\le i\le t.
$$
“Focused” means that adjoining $f$ completes the corresponding length-$(k+1)$ <arithmetic progression> if $f$ has its colour.

For $t=1$, put $F_1=2W(r,k)$. Find a <monochromatic> length-$k$ <arithmetic progression> in the first $W(r,k)$ points. Its next point is at most $2W(r,k)$ and supplies the focus. For $k=1$, give the selected singleton <common difference> $1$.

Suppose $F_t=N$ has been constructed. Set
$$
M=W(r^N,k),\qquad F_{t+1}=2NM.
$$
Divide $[2NM]$ into $2M$ consecutive blocks of length $N$. Colour the first $M$ block indices by their full <colour profiles>, that is, the ordered list of their $N$ colours. There are at most $r^N$ profiles, so the induction hypothesis on $k$ gives equally profiled blocks with indices
$$
j_0,j_0+D,\ldots,j_0+(k-1)D.
$$
When $k=1$, choose $D=1$. Otherwise $D>0$ and $j_0+kD\le2M$, since the last selected block is at most $M$ and $D\le M$.

Assume the original <finite colouring> contains no <monochromatic> length-$(k+1)$ <arithmetic progression>. Applying the property of $F_t$ to the common profile gives $t$ focused length-$k$ <arithmetic progressions> at a position $f$, with <common differences> $d_i$ and distinct colours $q_i$. The colour $q$ of position $f$ differs from every $q_i$: otherwise the profile already contains a length-$(k+1)$ <monochromatic> <arithmetic progression>, and so does any selected block.

Put $j_*=j_0+kD$ and $F=N(j_*-1)+f$, a point in the original interval. For $1\le h\le k$, the point
$$
F-h(ND+d_i)=N(j_*-hD-1)+(f-hd_i)
$$
lies in a selected block at profile position $f-hd_i$, so has colour $q_i$. Similarly $F-hND$ has colour $q$. Thus $t+1$ length-$k$ <arithmetic progressions> of distinct colours focus at $F$. This proves the step in $t$. At $t=r$, the focus necessarily has the colour of one of the $r$ progressions, giving a <monochromatic> length-$(k+1)$ <arithmetic progression>. Hence $W(r,k+1)$ exists, completing the <mathematical induction> and the proof of <Van der Waerden theorem>.

For the <partition regularity> deduction we first prove a strengthened progression lemma, the <Brauer progression theorem>: for positive $r,s,L$ with $L\ge2$, there is $B(r,s,L)$ such that every <finite colouring> of $[B(r,s,L)]$ using at most $r$ colours contains a <monochromatic set>
$$
\{sd,a,a+d,\ldots,a+(L-1)d\},\qquad a,d>0.
$$
Induct on $r$. For $r=1$, take $B(1,s,L)=\max(s,L)$ and $a=d=1$. Given the result for $r-1$, put
$$
N=B(r-1,s,L),\qquad K=(L-1)N+1,\qquad W=W(r,K),\qquad B(r,s,L)=\max(W,sNW).
$$
<Van der Waerden theorem> supplies a colour-$q$ <arithmetic progression> $a+j d_0$, $0\le j<K$, inside $[W]$. If some $sjd_0$, $1\le j\le N$, also has colour $q$, take $d=jd_0$ and use the $L$ terms $a,a+jd_0,\ldots,a+(L-1)jd_0$. They are in the progression because $(L-1)j\le K-1$.

Otherwise the <integers> $sjd_0$, $1\le j\le N$, use at most $r-1$ colours. Colour $j\in[N]$ by the colour of $sjd_0$ and apply the induction hypothesis. It supplies a <monochromatic set> $\{se,b,b+e,\ldots,b+(L-1)e\}$. Multiplying by $sd_0$ gives the required configuration with $a=sd_0b$ and $d=sd_0e$: its distinguished member is $s^2d_0e=sd$. All of these points are at most $sNd_0\le sNW$, so the finite bound is valid. This proves the <Brauer progression theorem> using only the just-proved <Van der Waerden theorem>.

A row is a <partition regular matrix> over the positive <integers> if every <finite colouring> of $\mathbb N$ admits a positive vector $x$ whose coordinates have one colour and whose product with the row is zero. Coordinates may repeat. Since the coefficients are <rational numbers>, multiplying them by a common positive denominator leaves the equation and every zero <subset> sum unchanged, so write the row as nonzero <integer> coefficients $b_1,\ldots,b_n$.

To prove necessity, suppose no nonempty <subset> of the $b_i$ sums to zero. Choose a <prime> $p>\sum_i|b_i|$ and use the <last nonzero digit coloring>
$$
c(x)=p^{-v_p(x)}x\pmod p\ \in\mathbb F_p^{\times},
$$
where $v_p$ is the <p-adic valuation>. If positive $x_i$ all had the same colour $u$ and satisfied $\sum_i b_ix_i=0$, let $m=\min_i v_p(x_i)$ and $I=\{i:v_p(x_i)=m\}$. After division by $p^m$ and reduction modulo $p$, the equation becomes
$$
u\sum_{i\in I}b_i=0\quad\text{in }\mathbb F_p.
$$
As $u\ne0$, this says $p\mid\sum_{i\in I}b_i$. The latter sum is nonzero and has absolute value at most $\sum_i|b_i|<p$, a contradiction. This explicitly proves the necessary obstruction without invoking a general <partition regularity> criterion.

Conversely, suppose $\varnothing\ne I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}$ and $\sum_{i\in I}b_i=0$. Select an index $j\in I$, and put
$$
S=\sum_{i\notin I}b_i,\qquad s=|b_j|,\qquad q=-\frac{sS}{b_j}\in\mathbb Z,\qquad u=\max(0,-q),\qquad L=\max(2,|q|+1).
$$
For any <finite colouring>, the <Brauer progression theorem> gives one-coloured $\{sd,a,a+d,\ldots,a+(L-1)d\}$. The indices $u$ and $u+q$ both lie between $0$ and $L-1$. Define positive, same-coloured coordinates by
$$
x_i=\begin{cases}
sd,&i\notin I,\\
a+ud,&i\in I\setminus\{j\},\\
a+(u+q)d,&i=j.
\end{cases}
$$
Then
$$
\sum_i b_ix_i=(a+ud)\sum_{i\in I}b_i+d(b_jq+sS)=0.
$$
This also covers $I$ equal to the whole index <set> and $q=0$. Reversing the clearing of denominators proves the exact criterion:
$$
\boxed{(a_1,\ldots,a_n)\text{ is partition regular}\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists\varnothing\ne I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}:\ \sum_{i\in I}a_i=0.}
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

An <ultrafilter> on $\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$ is a family $p\subseteq\mathcal P(\mathbb N)$ satisfying the proper <filter> axioms: $\mathbb N\in p$, $\varnothing\notin p$, <closure> under finite <intersections>, and upward <closure> under inclusion. In addition, for every $A\subseteq\mathbb N$, exactly one of $A$ and $A^c$ belongs to $p$. Equivalently, an <ultrafilter> is a maximal proper <filter>. The <principal ultrafilter> at $n$ consists of all <subsets> containing $n$; an <ultrafilter> not of this form is a <nonprincipal ultrafilter>.

To prove existence, start with the <cofinite filter> and order the proper <filters> containing it by inclusion. The <union> of a chain is still a proper <filter>: finitely many of its members lie in one <filter> of the chain, and no constituent contains the empty <set>. Thus <Zorn lemma> supplies a maximal such <filter> $p$. If $A\notin p$, adjoining $A$ cannot generate a proper <filter>; otherwise maximality fails. Consequently some $B\in p$ has $B\cap A=\varnothing$, so $A^c\in p$ by upward <closure>. This proves the <ultrafilter> alternative. No finite $A$ can belong to $p$, because $A^c$ is <cofinite> and already belongs to $p$. In particular $p$ contains no singleton and is a **<nonprincipal ultrafilter>**. The same argument, starting from any proper <filter>, proves the <ultrafilter lemma> used below.

Define $\beta\mathbb N$ to be the <set> of all <ultrafilters> on $\mathbb N$, with basic <open sets>
$$
\widehat A=\{p\in\beta\mathbb N:A\in p\},\qquad A\subseteq\mathbb N.
$$
They form a <topological basis> because $\widehat{\mathbb N}=\beta\mathbb N$ and $\widehat A\cap\widehat B=\widehat{A\cap B}$. Moreover $(\widehat A)^c=\widehat{A^c}$, so they are <clopen sets>. This is the <compact Hausdorff topology on ultrafilters>. If $p\ne q$, some $A$ belongs to $p$ but not $q$, and then $\widehat A$ and $\widehat{A^c}$ are disjoint open <neighbourhoods> of the two points. Thus $\beta\mathbb N$ is <Hausdorff>.

For <compactness>, take an <open cover> and refine it by basic <open sets> $\widehat{A_\lambda}$. If no finite subfamily covers, then for every finite collection of indices the <intersection> $\bigcap A_\lambda^c$ is nonempty. Indeed, if that <intersection> were empty, the corresponding $A_\lambda$ would cover $\mathbb N$, and every <ultrafilter> would contain at least one of them by the finite <ultrafilter> alternative. The complements therefore have the <finite intersection property> and generate a proper <filter>. Extend it to an <ultrafilter> $q$ by the proved <ultrafilter lemma>. Then $A_\lambda^c\in q$ for every $\lambda$, so $q$ belongs to none of the covering $\widehat{A_\lambda}$, a contradiction. A finite basic subcover lies inside a finite subcover of the original cover. Hence
$$
\boxed{\beta\mathbb N\text{ is compact and Hausdorff}.}
$$
The map $n\mapsto p_n$, where $p_n$ is the <principal ultrafilter> at $n$, identifies the discrete natural numbers with a <dense subset>: any nonempty $\widehat A$ has $A\ne\varnothing$ and contains $p_n$ for $n\in A$.

<Hindman theorem> states that every <finite colouring> of the positive <integers> admits an increasing <sequence> $x_1<x_2<\cdots$ whose <finite-sums set>
$$
FS(x_1,x_2,\ldots)=\left\{\sum_{i\in F}x_i:\varnothing\ne F\subseteq\mathbb N\text{ finite}\right\}
$$
is <monochromatic>. We now give the <idempotent-ultrafilter proof of Hindman's theorem>. For $A\subseteq\mathbb N$, write $A-x=\{y\in\mathbb N:x+y\in A\}$. The addition on $\beta\mathbb N$ is defined by
$$
A\in p+q\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad\{x\in\mathbb N:A-x\in q\}\in p.
$$
Take an <idempotent ultrafilter on the natural numbers> $p$, whose existence is allowed, so $p+p=p$. It is a <nonprincipal ultrafilter>: addition of principal points is $p_m+p_n=p_{m+n}$, whereas no positive <integer> satisfies $2m=m$.

Exactly one <colour class> $A$ belongs to $p$. Define
$$
A^*=A\cap\{x:A-x\in p\}.
$$
By idempotence, $A^*\in p$. The <idempotent-ultrafilter star-set lemma> needed for the recursion is that $A^*-x\in p$ whenever $x\in A^*$. To prove it, put $D=A-x\in p$. Idempotence again gives
$$
D^*=D\cap\{y:D-y\in p\}\in p.
$$
For $y\in D^*$, we have $x+y\in A$ and $A-(x+y)=D-y\in p$, hence $x+y\in A^*$. Thus $D^*\subseteq A^*-x$, proving the lemma by upward <closure>.

Choose $x_1\in A^*$. Suppose $F_n=FS(x_1,\ldots,x_n)\subseteq A^*$ has been constructed. Every factor of
$$
A^*\cap\bigcap_{s\in F_n}(A^*-s)\cap\left\{z:z>\sum_{i=1}^nx_i\right\}
$$
belongs to $p$: the translate factors do by the just-proved lemma, and the last factor does because it is <cofinite> and $p$ is a <nonprincipal ultrafilter>. The finite <intersection> is therefore nonempty. Choose $x_{n+1}$ in it. Then $x_{n+1}$ and every $s+x_{n+1}$ with $s\in F_n$ lie in $A^*$, so $F_{n+1}\subseteq A^*$. This constructs a strictly increasing <sequence>, indeed $x_{n+1}>\sum_{i\le n}x_i$, and proves <Hindman theorem>.

Finally apply <Hindman theorem> to obtain a <monochromatic> <finite-sums set> $FS(y_1,y_2,\ldots)$. We construct a <divisibility chain inside a finite-sums set> using disjoint successive blocks of indices. Start with $x_1=y_1$. If $x_i=m$, take $2m$ unused terms after all indices used so far, and split them into two groups of $m$ consecutive terms. In each group its $m+1$ <partial sums>, including $0$, contain two in the same <congruence class> modulo $m$ by the <pigeonhole principle>. Their difference is a nonempty consecutive sum divisible by $m$, and positivity makes it at least $m$. Let $x_{i+1}$ be the sum of these two selected sums. Then $m\mid x_{i+1}$ and $x_{i+1}\ge2m>m$.

The index <set> defining $x_{i+1}$ lies strictly after all previous index <sets>. Discard the other terms in these two groups before continuing. Because the chosen index <sets> are disjoint, every finite sum of distinct $x_i$ is a finite sum of distinct $y_j$, retaining the same colour. Therefore
$$
\boxed{x_1<x_2<\cdots,\qquad x_i\mid x_{i+1}\ \text{for every }i,\qquad FS(x_1,x_2,\ldots)\text{ is monochromatic}.}
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Write $[M]^\omega$ for the <set> of all infinite <subsets> of $M$. A family $Y\subseteq[\mathbb N]^\omega$ is a <Ramsey family in the homogeneous-cone sense> if there is an infinite $M$ such that either $[M]^\omega\subseteq Y$ or $[M]^\omega\cap Y=\varnothing$. Thus membership in $Y$ is constant on an infinite homogeneous cone. We will prove the stronger version for open families: such a cone exists inside every prescribed infinite ground <set>, as in the definition of a <Ramsey set of infinite subsets>.

The symbols in the question refer to the <ordinary topology on infinite subsets> $\tau$ and the <Ellentuck topology> $*$. For a <finite set> $s$, let
$$
[s]=\{X\in[\mathbb N]^\omega:s\text{ is the initial segment of }X\}.
$$
These <sets> form a <topological basis> for $\tau$, equivalently the <subspace topology> inherited from the <product topology> on $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N}$. For an infinite $A$ with $\max s<\min A$, the <sets>
$$
[s,A]=\{s\cup B:B\in[A]^\omega\}
$$
form a <topological basis> for $*$; take $\max\varnothing=0$. In particular $[s]=[s,\{n:n>\max s\}]$, so $*$ is finer than $\tau$.

For a non-Ramsey example use the <finite-symmetric-difference parity colouring>. On $[\mathbb N]^\omega$, define the <equivalence relation> $X\sim Z$ if their <symmetric difference> $X\mathbin\triangle Z$ is finite. By the <axiom of choice>, select a representative $R$ in every <equivalence class>. Give $X$ the colour
$$
c(X)=|X\mathbin\triangle R_X|\pmod2,
$$
where $R_X$ is its class representative. Deleting one point stays in the same class and toggles the parity. Thus for every infinite $M$, the <sets> $M$ and $M\setminus\{\min M\}$ are both in $[M]^\omega$ but have opposite colours. Either <colour class>, regarded as a family $Y$, is therefore **not Ramsey**.

Now let $U$ be $\tau$-open. We prove that for every infinite $A_0$ there is an infinite $C\subseteq A_0$ with $[C]^\omega\subseteq U$ or $[C]^\omega\cap U=\varnothing$. The proof uses <acceptance and rejection of finite stems>, which we define explicitly. For a <finite stem> $s$ and an infinite tail $A$ above $\max s$, say that $A$ accepts $s$ if $[s,A]\subseteq U$, and rejects $s$ if no infinite <subset> of $A$ accepts $s$. Both properties persist on taking infinite <subsets>. Every tail has an infinite <subset> that decides $s$: take an accepting <subset> if one exists, and otherwise it already rejects $s$.

First thin $A_0$ to decide the empty stem. Then construct $b_1<b_2<\cdots$ and nested infinite tails. At step $n$, choose $b_n$ from the current tail, discard its points at most $b_n$, and successively thin what remains to decide every stem $s\subseteq\{b_1,\ldots,b_n\}$. There are only finitely many such stems, so an infinite tail remains. Let $B=\{b_1,b_2,\ldots\}$ and write $B/s=\{b\in B:b>\max s\}$. For every finite $s\subseteq B$, the tail $B/s$ decides $s$, since it lies in the tail obtained at the stage when the largest point of $s$ was selected. The empty stem is also decided. This is <deciding all finite stems by fusion>.

If $B$ accepts the empty stem, then $[B]^\omega\subseteq U$, as required. Otherwise $B$ rejects it. We need the <finitely many accepting extensions of a rejected stem> observation. Suppose a tail $B/s$ rejects $s$. Only finitely many $a\in B/s$ can have $B/a$ accepting $s\cup\{a\}$. Indeed, if infinitely many such $a$ formed a <set> $D$, every member of $[s,D]$ would have some first new point $a$ and all later points in $B/a$, hence would lie in $U$. This would make $D$ an accepting <subset> of $B/s$, contradicting rejection.

Choose $c_1<c_2<\cdots$ in $B$ recursively so that for every finite $s\subseteq\{c_1,\ldots,c_n\}$, the tail $B/s$ rejects $s$. The empty stem satisfies this initially. At the next step, each of the finitely many previous stems has only finitely many accepting one-point extensions by the observation. Choose $c_{n+1}$ larger than all previous points and outside all those finite exceptional <sets>. The first fusion ensured that the tail for every new stem decides it; since it does not accept it, it rejects it. This maintains the recursion.

Put $C=\{c_1,c_2,\ldots\}$. If some $X\in[C]^\omega$ belonged to $U$, ordinary openness would give a finite initial segment $s$ of $X$ with $[s]\subseteq U$. In particular $[s,C/s]\subseteq U$. But $C/s$ is an infinite <subset> of $B/s$, which rejects $s$, a contradiction. Hence $[C]^\omega\cap U=\varnothing$. We have proved
$$
\boxed{\text{Every }\tau\text{-open family is Ramsey, even inside every infinite ground set}.}
$$

= i
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take the <cone on an infinite coinfinite ground set>
$$
\boxed{E=[2\mathbb N]^\omega.}
$$
It is $*$-open because $E=[\varnothing,2\mathbb N]$ is a basic <open set> of the <Ellentuck topology>. It is not $\tau$-open: if $X\in E$, every ordinary basic <neighbourhood> $[s]$ of $X$ contains an infinite extension of $s$ having an odd point larger than $\max s$. That extension is outside $E$. Therefore no point of $E$ has an ordinary open <neighbourhood> contained in $E$.

= ii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The same <cone on an infinite coinfinite ground set> works:
$$
\boxed{E=[2\mathbb N]^\omega.}
$$
It is $\tau$-closed: a <set> outside $E$ has an odd point, and the ordinary <neighbourhood> given by its initial segment through that point remains outside $E$. Its $\tau$-<interior> is empty, since every ordinary basic <open set> admits an extension having an odd point. Hence its ordinary <closure> equals itself and has empty <interior>, proving that it is a $\tau$-<nowhere dense set>.

In the <Ellentuck topology>, however, $E$ is itself a nonempty <open set>. Its $*$-<closure> therefore has nonempty $*$-<interior>, so $E$ is **not $*$-nowhere dense**.

= iii
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let
$$
\boxed{D=\{X\subseteq\mathbb N:X\text{ is cofinite}\}.}
$$
This is the <dense countable family of cofinite infinite subsets>. Every ordinary basic <open set> $[s]$ contains the <cofinite set> $s\cup\{n:n>\max s\}$. Thus $D$ is $\tau$-dense: its ordinary <closure> is the whole space, so it is **not $\tau$-nowhere dense**.

It is $*$-closed. Indeed, for an infinite noncofinite $X$, the <Ellentuck topology> <neighbourhood> $[\varnothing,X]$ contains only noncofinite <sets>, because every <subset> of $X$ misses the infinite <complement> of $X$. This <neighbourhood> is disjoint from $D$.

Finally every nonempty basic $*$-<open set> $[s,A]$ contains a smaller basic $*$-<open set> disjoint from $D$: enumerate $A$ and let $B$ consist of every second point. Then $A\setminus B$ is infinite, and $[s,B]\subseteq[s,A]$ contains no <cofinite set>. In particular $D$ has empty $*$-<interior>. As it is already $*$-closed, its $*$-<closure> has empty $*$-<interior>, proving that it is **$*$-nowhere dense**.
